{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 220,
    "dDurationMs": 4619,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrinos are tiny, almost weightless particles\nthat only interact via gravity and nuclear"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4839,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5839,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because they don't interact electromagnetically,\nthat is, with light, they literally can't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9280,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be seen!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10280,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, detecting a neutrino is kind of like\ntrying to catch a bullet with a butterfly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 13900,
    "dDurationMs": 4859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "net – a beam of neutrinos will travel through\nlead for two years before it stops."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 18759,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "(in comparison, radiation from nuclear fallout\ncan be blocked by about ten cm of lead)"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23650,
    "dDurationMs": 1490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do you detect a neutrino?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25140,
    "dDurationMs": 4899,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One common way is to fill a big tank with\nwater: we know light slows down through water,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30039,
    "dDurationMs": 3531,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if a neutrino with enough energy happens\nto knock into an electron, the electron will"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33570,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "zip through the water faster than the light\ndoes!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36000,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When this happens, the electron gives off\na weak glow called Cherenkov Radiation - it's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40270,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "kind of like a sonic boom for light, and it\nallows us to detect the neutrino."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44640,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The biggest neutrino detector in the world\nis a balloon over the south pole that actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48280,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uses the whole antarctic ice sheet as its\ntank of water!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51580,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Neutrinos also tell us that the universe is\nnot the same as its mirror image."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54190,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you switch left with right, clockwise with\ncounterclockwise, almost all of physics, like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59320,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity, electromagnetism, and the strong\nnuclear force, is unchanged."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63270,
    "dDurationMs": 4050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, the weird thing about neutrinos is\nthat in physics terms, they're all left-handed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67320,
    "dDurationMs": 2110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- their mirror image doesn't exist!"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 69430,
    "dDurationMs": 8570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So neutrinos are the vampires of physics."
    } ]
  } ]
}
